uestion/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x-y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-7y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335639/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335639"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335470/selection/76e765cfedacf8a1a029340f6dc66ccdd3f43e6709ec62d5024f2d9c142d4156" :answer/id #uuid "290f356d-3f9b-554d-804c-f131cd0fd4f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335470/selection/1efa141576b43ab0d86e22c56c32f89c9b3f3912d330d6580b836a3c9d21585d" :answer/id #uuid "fe4d2050-7c44-5f30-b92e-356be9d07fa5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335470/selection/a92ccc5da25163c5001846db3e24f63ef50bff716c23a65e3cc6b99139206448" :answer/id #uuid "3b9306af-9f87-58ec-8c7f-fdbe49283a22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335470/selection/1b1290d587154cec04914d4694c7c3dbb1f9fd5919d275d3f05e8fafe7ddcfff" :answer/id #uuid "3fde1925-6b36-5327-ab25-206019e09fae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335470/selection/8ce90e03eebf0f8975116e3f9f5f8ad077a5d64ae7996b6aa9c83037e9b54007" :answer/id #uuid "1a301477-8cae-5d27-8b48-f50181e7e9bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335470/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2a16de5-d93c-5668-b2d1-ff3a5faa12ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335470/selection/1efa141576b43ab0d86e22c56c32f89c9b3f3912d330d6580b836a3c9d21585d" :answer-field/choices ["q-335470/selection/76e765cfedacf8a1a029340f6dc66ccdd3f43e6709ec62d5024f2d9c142d4156" "q-335470/selection/1efa141576b43ab0d86e22c56c32f89c9b3f3912d330d6580b836a3c9d21585d" "q-335470/selection/a92ccc5da25163c5001846db3e24f63ef50bff716c23a65e3cc6b99139206448" "q-335470/selection/1b1290d587154cec04914d4694c7c3dbb1f9fd5919d275d3f05e8fafe7ddcfff" "q-335470/selection/8ce90e03eebf0f8975116e3f9f5f8ad077a5d64ae7996b6aa9c83037e9b54007"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335470" :question/id #uuid "eac2bde7-7ca3-515f-8106-3f78aefe257b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335470" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1=0 \\\\ 2x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335470/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335470"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186221896 :question/answer-fields 17592186220316] {:db/id "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/47a42c75a49cec12cca087bc9a82383ecebbf5d9fc0b100d45f34d37115c9aed" :answer/id #uuid "412d02da-d8de-5b76-9883-b9292a63e71c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/06b91b7fe790560f73a4110e035b2c912f63b3e3137a43b5542b4c65565b34db" :answer/id #uuid "8fc71ead-7e89-5167-bf85-550e4a6e99e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/406ca8bd2e109dc916d73ef05a555b3192649d6cd9855289a0abf2cada740b70" :answer/id #uuid "666e0ff7-33f9-5005-8ef5-eb03ad305c35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/aadb013c10f8a998bd6bcfb4426480c05ea550a0a8e3dfef2793004740d47af2" :answer/id #uuid "1a200f94-9fab-56dd-8824-e6c3e2e8797a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/408e6eeec93a6feedbd3a712144e979cba6de93df4ac7070188f81de4eba1572" :answer/id #uuid "88f796b8-628e-57bf-8c52-1c57678c15e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "b665653f-5025-5f24-bb8b-df34a10441a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/06b91b7fe790560f73a4110e035b2c912f63b3e3137a43b5542b4c65565b34db" :answer-field/choices ["q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/47a42c75a49cec12cca087bc9a82383ecebbf5d9fc0b100d45f34d37115c9aed" "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/06b91b7fe790560f73a4110e035b2c912f63b3e3137a43b5542b4c65565b34db" "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/406ca8bd2e109dc916d73ef05a555b3192649d6cd9855289a0abf2cada740b70" "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/aadb013c10f8a998bd6bcfb4426480c05ea550a0a8e3dfef2793004740d47af2" "q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb/408e6eeec93a6feedbd3a712144e979cba6de93df4ac7070188f81de4eba1572"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333360"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3y-3=0\\,⇒\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+1=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333360/selection/ma-v1/f707f2dba803407ffa093e671f239262b541064a322f73c21f864a56f5ad17eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-335558/selection/86fd1d927821ca7f876d739cd692b372dfc66a37825ed9b2955b58a4b3fe7062" :answer/id #uuid "1cf93f14-563c-5796-9154-0c5613daf3d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335558/selection/23a1d7025ecf342598ad3e09f648c0c09c833202e4c753ebb43af5ba7b406ff5" :answer/id #uuid "6c0a19de-d64b-5849-abd5-8c6343460b3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335558/selection/4138a7b41716f32e551f11048dda978c6406f540c561982c23955e8d608c6fa6" :answer/id #uuid "11af0954-29ac-5c4b-b021-fca42a152f31" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335558/selection/0ba1072368e260f24ad3e2a4e1ae991a9192b3afaa2b880988ccc3eaf12607be" :answer/id #uuid "0e78d487-79b6-5de2-b365-3c200797c2aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335558/selection/61a8b1feeb0b2d4ab261861d7283ec38025572b74dbeb808c143489e78d56753" :answer/id #uuid "7de20d88-bc1f-5916-af50-3eb6cc255eb3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335558/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb26ac33-1571-50cd-aa57-eb7e5c01f475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335558/selection/61a8b1feeb0b2d4ab261861d7283ec38025572b74dbeb808c143489e78d56753" :answer-field/choices ["q-335558/selection/86fd1d927821ca7f876d739cd692b372dfc66a37825ed9b2955b58a4b3fe7062" "q-335558/selection/23a1d7025ecf342598ad3e09f648c0c09c833202e4c753ebb43af5ba7b406ff5" "q-335558/selection/4138a7b41716f32e551f11048dda978c6406f540c561982c23955e8d608c6fa6" "q-335558/selection/0ba1072368e260f24ad3e2a4e1ae991a9192b3afaa2b880988ccc3eaf12607be" "q-335558/selection/61a8b1feeb0b2d4ab261861d7283ec38025572b74dbeb808c143489e78d56753"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335558" :question/id #uuid "a345a07a-9a32-5621-bbdf-168369d7d55d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335558" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ x(4x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get $y=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-335558/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335558"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332968/selection/823d98279246a9da6066e7441ba45155a57ba63860127db18437ca4bef092f80" :answer/id #uuid "b6dd0d14-19d1-562f-aa3e-ac0e4eb03323" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332968/selection/ff8bd54e404fae5277f5a203cf839cd25ca63f509d6138bc779b1a531f6d5401" :answer/id #uuid "78164657-caae-5d95-91d8-de016269a117" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332968/selection/5fcc118ac82cc8d2fe9accb7fe0b6beb659544c7eda164ee94fcd2364104fe80" :answer/id #uuid "1a3802a9-0f54-53e4-8f51-5dd89c864e42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions converge to the equilibrium only if the system is linear." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332968/selection/f50a0b4fb9608e3860b307dafd13b3054b9f79b218c060c8122c0d90934f8d76" :answer/id #uuid "da013dea-3275-5479-9c64-6fb363497cb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions approach the equilibrium only along specific trajectories." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332968/selection/6c47b6c954c7f152c4e492f4f76225dfc2cdf3b5b6ed263d5fd6adb2c3d21252" :answer/id #uuid "19426713-b39c-53f9-99cf-6429cbc787c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332968/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b35eed4d-7668-54c5-ad4e-fb519364521c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332968/selection/6c47b6c954c7f152c4e492f4f76225dfc2cdf3b5b6ed263d5fd6adb2c3d21252" :answer-field/choices ["q-332968/selection/823d98279246a9da6066e7441ba45155a57ba63860127db18437ca4bef092f80" "q-332968/selection/ff8bd54e404fae5277f5a203cf839cd25ca63f509d6138bc779b1a531f6d5401" "q-332968/selection/5fcc118ac82cc8d2fe9accb7fe0b6beb659544c7eda164ee94fcd2364104fe80" "q-332968/selection/f50a0b4fb9608e3860b307dafd13b3054b9f79b218c060c8122c0d90934f8d76" "q-332968/selection/6c47b6c954c7f152c4e492f4f76225dfc2cdf3b5b6ed263d5fd6adb2c3d21252"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332968" :question/id #uuid "6b6b058e-9ff1-5f78-9443-7901f71ac9f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332968" :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332968/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332968"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-1/ffa15369997b33c0bb21977a1092ee9fba9af65ae23da23d857d94b0fb669c73" :answer/id #uuid "4cd3f73b-6552-5376-a1b4-6c2dead84c81" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Some" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-1/e1381ae7c7a92b4aa7f0a2bd7d1adabb4c69d577c3ac304b32f6f859826b96b8" :answer/id #uuid "beb7bdeb-3698-506a-a17a-649ec6a473e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a12cfc8-d704-5483-a81b-4422b2e0187e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-335460/field-1/ffa15369997b33c0bb21977a1092ee9fba9af65ae23da23d857d94b0fb669c73" :answer-field/choices ["q-335460/field-1/ffa15369997b33c0bb21977a1092ee9fba9af65ae23da23d857d94b0fb669c73" "q-335460/field-1/e1381ae7c7a92b4aa7f0a2bd7d1adabb4c69d577c3ac304b32f6f859826b96b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-2/79743ba2cf4897f08c2f687f8ca9198b4663730a575f74649413a8c994320d18" :answer/id #uuid "c5449319-7870-58a0-aff3-bb57740002c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-2/b75bc05c3a8aae4f5f3c2899162126840a25b8f7eda57f1ef199e4a16f56fba4" :answer/id #uuid "3500047e-2f42-5664-a968-66fcf2aab66d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "far from" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0dcab76-0870-5503-be1f-3635a35b5878" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-335460/field-2/79743ba2cf4897f08c2f687f8ca9198b4663730a575f74649413a8c994320d18" :answer-field/choices ["q-335460/field-2/79743ba2cf4897f08c2f687f8ca9198b4663730a575f74649413a8c994320d18" "q-335460/field-2/b75bc05c3a8aae4f5f3c2899162126840a25b8f7eda57f1ef199e4a16f56fba4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-3/8be2d115fa7b976b4c32465b7251c47b1a3e5672c0a4d8557f26e06f92eb40f8" :answer/id #uuid "e23e98ad-aaa7-5b25-a153-f9483a81e08c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "remain close to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-3/d3d064377015e2100502a8ca521489d27d4a570be9709cc9238160c7c0343862" :answer/id #uuid "8c9c0a1e-2249-5209-85a0-854d3d6d7dcf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "49bfdbba-117c-5ca8-98ef-0fc9f3ac2c0d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-335460/field-3/d3d064377015e2100502a8ca521489d27d4a570be9709cc9238160c7c0343862" :answer-field/choices ["q-335460/field-3/8be2d115fa7b976b4c32465b7251c47b1a3e5672c0a4d8557f26e06f92eb40f8" "q-335460/field-3/d3d064377015e2100502a8ca521489d27d4a570be9709cc9238160c7c0343862"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335460" :question/id #uuid "d4f31534-294a-5c66-bb7a-bbd9c8ed5e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-335460" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **unstable** equilibrium point.{{field-1}} solutions that start\n{{field-2}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-3}}\nthe equilibrium." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n$\\text{Some}$ solutions that start $\\text{arbitrarily close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{eventually move away from}$ the equilibrium." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335460/field-1" "q-335460/field-2" "q-335460/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335460"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333385"] :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Unstable.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-335465/selection/f90af16d53b64f18d625ff3eace399df056a4e71194606e0c73388c7e67a5f9c" :answer/id #uuid "c0ca0e9b-d61e-5c9e-a3e6-56e9bc0fbccd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335465/selection/fcda0b7bf263dd68eee7b8c102aa23e51e5c82e7cc8286ce388d069475b06d03" :answer/id #uuid "22ff3695-5149-575b-9a4a-78208dae0586" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for nonlinear systems." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335465/selection/efa085861614fb969d9d1eeb912156eeea84a9f563c14f4b3ebaea31dc8dd746" :answer/id #uuid "9d1a3bdf-2e9a-55ce-80ff-de9408d3d51b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Asymptotically stable." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335465/selection/8de973a8b722c5184b720214154411f6f9a4bc3509a1d33cca03e3004992ee9d" :answer/id #uuid "d7ffea3e-b54a-599a-a784-6d10da09dad4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Unstable." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335465/selection/8b2a7028f5aa36c1e156bd853bab926afde3dd2a67833b838238ef737e7abe18" :answer/id #uuid "db6c711e-a164-50b5-bfec-66eb3cd983c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for certain initial conditions." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335465/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac1cde9-39f2-5443-a24a-e79a254128b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335465/selection/f90af16d53b64f18d625ff3eace399df056a4e71194606e0c73388c7e67a5f9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-335465/selection/f90af16d53b64f18d625ff3eace399df056a4e71194606e0c73388c7e67a5f9c" "q-335465/selection/fcda0b7bf263dd68eee7b8c102aa23e51e5c82e7cc8286ce388d069475b06d03" "q-335465/selection/efa085861614fb969d9d1eeb912156eeea84a9f563c14f4b3ebaea31dc8dd746" "q-335465/selection/8de973a8b722c5184b720214154411f6f9a4bc3509a1d33cca03e3004992ee9d" "q-335465/selection/8b2a7028f5aa36c1e156bd853bab926afde3dd2a67833b838238ef737e7abe18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335465" :question/id #uuid "c2b4530a-6c6c-5e8b-9d00-bf27eb10da96" :question/math-academy-id "q-335465" :question/problem "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to remain in a bounded region around the equilibrium, sometimes oscillating or circling, but they do not converge to the equilibrium as time increases. How should the equilibrium be classified?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Stable but not asymptotically stable.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335465/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335465"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335458/field-1/d75f4c3d0456929d4b5eb03d13a5a4b20f2488964360e08afc832e57e4631f77" :answer/id #uuid "a5afa473-578e-5078-b850-3d0a17657cfa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "away from" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335458/field-1/b80c7a4265c20f078563e10946264f3cbe32351e845341ec0ceea9e476ccb50a" :answer/id #uuid "c86631ba-5345-569f-85c1-a80b34b80210" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "close to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335458/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3517b8ea-67d1-585b-b3ba-b6be985baf15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-335458/field-1/b80c7a4265c20f078563e10946264f3cbe32351e845341ec0ceea9e476ccb50a" :answer-field/choices ["q-335458/field-1/d75f4c3d0456929d4b5eb03d13a5a4b20f2488964360e08afc832e57e4631f77" "q-335458/field-1/b80c7a4265c20f078563e10946264f3cbe32351e845341ec0ceea9e476ccb50a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335458/field-2/c1a0e57438a2f8deb046f0e83c4ea92521be6919953a12b3c6df237a575be04c" :answer/id #uuid "3331fc98-4779-5ae7-a4b7-1c92d49e0ecb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "but move away from" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335458/field-2/39e1d361cd0a5185dff9e6b105112c2cc12946089820fea29a01a55e2b0099ba" :answer/id #uuid "54319469-2674-5653-b925-ae0a442a6905" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/val