 :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\to 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335458/field-4/4d0e09924b0d58e8bf730b772c2750eed10c3dc8403ce3b65cb53b6b8398e12c" :answer/id #uuid "7cbc2a80-9ed0-54b1-9d3f-63cf07946d7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\to \\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335458/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "be17fc75-fd6d-57cd-89a4-717b135a1d68" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-335458/field-4/4d0e09924b0d58e8bf730b772c2750eed10c3dc8403ce3b65cb53b6b8398e12c" :answer-field/choices ["q-335458/field-4/c95415f247381b2d3b2eec9c371a5f1c3cf97b6e737c6182dcbc51f0f6780858" "q-335458/field-4/4d0e09924b0d58e8bf730b772c2750eed10c3dc8403ce3b65cb53b6b8398e12c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335458" :question/id #uuid "3889c218-203d-525e-b513-cc653db33652" :question/math-academy-id "q-335458" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point.The solutions that start sufficiently \n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium\n{{field-2}} \nthe \n{{field-3}} \nas \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium $\\text{and converge to}$ the $\\text{equilibrium}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335458/field-1" "q-335458/field-2" "q-335458/field-3" "q-335458/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21665"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/assets/9cff385cda8e9a4cac784cb4212fee9ec1e65f48a1ebd05f9af75f71aead66aa.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21666"] :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n- Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
